Given state space model, \[\frac{d}{dt}
\begin{bmatrix}
x_{1}\\
x_{2}\\
x_{3}
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
0 &amp; a_{1} &amp; 0\\
0 &amp; 0 &amp; a_{2}\\
a_{3} &amp; 0 &amp; 0
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
x_{1}\\
x_{2}\\
x_{3}
\end{bmatrix}
+
\begin{bmatrix}
0\\
0\\
1
\end{bmatrix}u\] \[y=
\begin{bmatrix}
1 &amp; 0 &amp; 0
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
x_{1}\\
x_{2}\\
x_{3}
\end{bmatrix}\] Therefore, \[A=
\begin{bmatrix}
0 &amp; a_{1} &amp; 0\\
0 &amp; 0 &amp; a_{2}\\
a_{3} &amp; 0 &amp; 0
\end{bmatrix},
\qquad
B=
\begin{bmatrix}
0\\
0\\
1
\end{bmatrix}\] The controllability matrix is \[Q_c=
\begin{bmatrix}
B &amp; AB &amp; A^2B
\end{bmatrix}\] First, compute \(AB\): \[AB=
\begin{bmatrix}
0 &amp; a_{1} &amp; 0\\
0 &amp; 0 &amp; a_{2}\\
a_{3} &amp; 0 &amp; 0
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
0\\
0\\
1
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
0\\
a_{2}\\
0
\end{bmatrix}\] Next, compute \(A^2B\): \[A^2B=A(AB)\] \[=
\begin{bmatrix}
0 &amp; a_{1} &amp; 0\\
0 &amp; 0 &amp; a_{2}\\
a_{3} &amp; 0 &amp; 0
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
0\\
a_{2}\\
0
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
a_{1}a_{2}\\
0\\
0
\end{bmatrix}\] Hence, \[Q_c=
\begin{bmatrix}
0 &amp; 0 &amp; a_{1}a_{2}\\
0 &amp; a_{2} &amp; 0\\
1 &amp; 0 &amp; 0
\end{bmatrix}\] Its determinant is \[|Q_c|
=
-a_{1}a_{2}^{2}\] For complete controllability, \[|Q_c|\neq 0\] \[-a_{1}a_{2}^{2}\neq 0\] \[\therefore\qquad
a_{1}\neq 0,
\qquad
a_{2}\neq 0\] Observe that \(a_{3}\) does not appear in the controllability determinant. Therefore, controllability is independent of \(a_{3}\). \[\boxed{(D)\;a_{1}\neq 0,\; a_{2}\neq 0,\; a_{3}=0}\]

This sentence tests the distinction between the verbs "lie" (to recline) and "lay" (to place an object down flat). <ul><li>The intransitive verb meaning to recline has the principal parts: Present = lie, Past = lay, Past Participle = lain.</li><li>Since the action took place in the past ("till 8 o'clock"), we must use the simple past form of the intransitive verb lie, which is <strong>lay</strong>.</li><li>Option A uses "laid", which is the past tense of the transitive verb lay (requires a direct object). Option B uses the non-standard word "layed". Option C uses the past participle "lain" incorrectly in a simple past context.</li></ul> \[\boxed{\text{D:  He lay in bed till 8 o’clock in the morning.}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li><strong>Lie vs. Lay Principal Forms</strong>: \[\begin{aligned}
    \text{Intransitive (Recline): } &amp;\text{lie} \longrightarrow \text{\textbf{lay}} \longrightarrow \text{lain} \\
    \text{Transitive (Put down): } &amp;\text{lay} \longrightarrow \text{laid} \longrightarrow \text{laid}
    \end{aligned}\]</li></ul></div> <div class="sol-block sol-mistakes"><div class="sol-block-title">Mistakes to avoid</div><ul><li>Never use "laid" when describing a subject that is reclining or resting in the past tense without a direct object.</li></ul></div>

Let us decouple the alpha sequence terms into independent tracking columns for the first and second letters: \[\text{Terms: } \mathbf{AD}, \quad \mathbf{CG}, \quad \mathbf{FK}, \quad \mathbf{JP}, \quad \dots\] <strong>Step 1: Analyze the sequence of the first letters (A, C, F, J, ...)</strong><br/>Convert the letters to their respective numerical positions in the alphabet (A = 1, C = 3, F = 6, J = 10): \[\begin{aligned}
\text{A} &amp;\xrightarrow{+2} \text{C} \\
\text{C} &amp;\xrightarrow{+3} \text{F} \\
\text{F} &amp;\xrightarrow{+4} \text{J}
\end{aligned}\] Following this incremental pattern \(+2, +3, +4, \dots\), the next leap must be \(+5\): \[\text{J (10)} \xrightarrow{+5} 15 \implies \mathbf{O}\] <strong>Step 2: Analyze the sequence of the second letters (D, G, K, P, ...)</strong><br/>Convert the letters to their respective numerical positions (D = 4, G = 7, K = 11, P = 16): \[\begin{aligned}
\text{D} &amp;\xrightarrow{+3} \text{G} \\
\text{G} &amp;\xrightarrow{+4} \text{K} \\
\text{K} &amp;\xrightarrow{+5} \text{P}
\end{aligned}\] Following this incremental pattern \(+3, +4, +5, \dots\), the next leap must be \(+6\): \[\text{P (16)} \xrightarrow{+6} 22 \implies \mathbf{V}\] Combining the next predicted coordinates yields the term **OV**. \[\boxed{\text{A: OV}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li><strong>Decoupled Alpha Series</strong>: Complex letter patterns are best resolved by separating the tracking positions into independent numerical index series (\(1,2,\dots,26\)) to detect underlying arithmetic increments.</li></ul></div>

The slope (\(m\)) of a straight line passing through two coordinate points \((X_1, Y_1)\) and \((X_2, Y_2)\) is given by the formula: \[m = \dfrac{Y_2 - Y_1}{X_2 - X_1}\] Given the points \((4, p)\) and \((0, q)\), and the slope \(m = \dfrac{3}{4}\), we substitute these values into the slope formula: \[\begin{aligned}
\dfrac{q - p}{0 - 4} &amp;= \dfrac{3}{4} \\[1.5ex]
\dfrac{q - p}{-4} &amp;= \dfrac{3}{4}
\end{aligned}\] Multiplying both sides by \(-4\): \[\begin{aligned}
q - p &amp;= \dfrac{3}{4} \times (-4) \\[1.5ex]
q - p &amp;= -3
\end{aligned}\] To isolate the value of \((p - q)\), we multiply the entire equation by \(-1\): \[-(q - p) = -(-3) \implies p - q = 3\] \[\boxed{\text{C:3 }}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li><strong>Coordinate Slope Inversion</strong>: Pay close attention to negative signs during algebraic expansion. If the denominator is negative (\(X_2 - X_1 = -4\)), it reverses the sign of your target variable difference group.</li></ul></div>
